  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 358 AM EDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\n- Showers and storms possible today\n\nA cold front is situated from Northern Lake Michigan extending\nsouthward to near Chicago and then to the southwest into portions\nof southeast Iowa. Along and ahead of the front dew points have\nsurged to near 70f. MUCAPE values remain well over 1000 j/kg.\nConvection continues to develop ahead of the front over Southern\nLake Michigan. So far, the convection has been rather tame. These\nstorms and additional activity that forms will work its way across\nSouthwest Lower Michigan this morning. Most of the activity will\nbe over by 16Z or so. We are not expecting severe weather today,\nbut some locally heavy rain could occur given PWAT values around\n1.5 inches. We should see fair weather from this afternoon through\ntonight.\n\n- Strong storms possible Sunday and Monday\n\nStrong to severe storms are possible both on Sunday and Monday\nacross portions of Southwest Lower Michigan. On Sunday, the cold\nfront that moves through this afternoon and tonight, slows off to\nour south and returns north as a warm front. The warm front will\nbe the driving mechanism for thunderstorm initiation. There is\nfairly high confidence amongst the members of the HREF in bringing\nan MCS through the region during the afternoon and early evening. \nThere could be multiple rounds of showers and storms Sunday as a \n25-35 knot LLJ impinges on the warm front. Organized storms are\nlikely due to a mid level jet which leads to increasing deep layer\nshear to in excess of 40 knots by evening. The details and\nmagnitude of the threat will be determined by how convection\ndevelops upstream tonight into Sunday morning. SPC has much of our\nsouthern CWA in a marginal threat for severe weather. We will be\nmonitoring trends in the forecast as we head into tomorrow.\n\nAnother threat exists for severe weather on Monday as the warm\nfront stalls over the area. The low level jet is situated over\nSouthern Lower Michigan and will likely continue to be a focus for\nlift as it encounters the warm front over our Southern CWA. A\nSlight risk for severe weather exists on Monday roughly over our\nsouthern half of the forecast area or from I-96 to the south.\nCould be a second day of rounds of strong storms. \n\nPWAT values will approach 2.0 inches over next two days and with\ntraining of precipitation we could see some localized flooding.",
